---
import InsightArticle from '../../layouts/InsightArticle.astro';
import WorkedTable from '../../components/insights/WorkedTable.astro';
import PractitionerNote from '../../components/insights/PractitionerNote.astro';
import { practitionerB2 } from '../../data/practitioner-b2';
import examples from '../../data/insights/2026-11-14-operations.json';
const e = practitionerB2[2];
---
<InsightArticle title={e.title} description={e.description} date="2026-11-14" type="Explainer" readTime="5 min" standfirst="An extractor can return a perfectly shaped object with the wrong amount inside it. Track the response, the schema and the evidence as separate checks.">
  <p>Imagine a document containing the amount 42.00. Your schema requires a numeric amount and a currency. A response containing 24.00 can pass the type check. It still fails the job. No model benchmark is needed to see the gap: the schema describes the permitted shape, while the source document determines the correct value.</p>
  <p>Structured outputs are useful for defining that shape. Anthropic's <a href={e.sources[0].url}>documentation</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes constrained JSON output, supported-schema limits and exceptions for refusals and token-limit stops. Our proposed reporting method keeps those outcomes separate from factual acceptance.</p>
  <h2>Stop counting HTTP success as task success</h2>
  <p>The documentation says a refusal can arrive with HTTP status 200 and need not match the requested schema. A response cut short at max_tokens can also be incomplete. Inspect the completion status before treating the body as an answer to your task.</p>
  <p>Then parse and validate it. After that, check the values against the document and the job's rules. Those stages give you a more useful failure record than one success flag. They also tell you where to spend effort: a malformed result needs different attention from an accurately formatted wrong answer.</p>
  <WorkedTable headers={['Cumulative gate in a fictional run', 'Passing requests', 'Share of original run']} rows={examples.validation} caption="Invented nested counts out of 1,000 completed HTTP requests. These illustrate reporting gates, not observed provider error rates." />
  <p>In the example, 970 requests pass the schema and 900 pass the task check. Task acceptance is 90% of the original run, or about 92.8% of the schema-valid subset. Both statements are true, but they answer different questions. Publish the denominator beside the percentage.</p>
  <h2>Write the evidence rule before the prompt</h2>
  <p>For each extracted field, decide what supports it. A simple document might let you retain a page reference and the exact text span. A calculated total may need the input lines and the calculation. Keep evidence that a reviewer can inspect, rather than accepting the model's confidence as proof.</p>
  <p>Decide how to represent missing information. If the amount is absent, an explicit missing value can be the correct result. A schema that demands a number without allowing an absence state makes it harder to express that outcome. Test the schema with absent and ambiguous fields before running a large collection.</p>
  <p>Also define the normalisation rules. Should a date with no year remain incomplete? May the extractor convert a currency symbol into a code? Which decimal notation is allowed? These are application decisions. Preserve the original text alongside the normalised value during testing so the conversion can be checked.</p>
  <h2>Retry according to what failed</h2>
  <p>Keep a refusal as a refusal. A blind retry loop obscures it and adds spend. For an incomplete result, inspect the limit and request size before trying again. For a schema-valid wrong value, more output space does not address the evidence failure. Review the prompt, document handling and acceptance rule.</p>
  <p>Assign a final result to each original job even if it has several attempts. Count the spend on those attempts together. Otherwise the tracker can report a high pass rate by hiding the failed requests that made the answer possible.</p>
  <p>Test the awkward documents deliberately: two different totals, a revised version, a table continued on another page, a field that is mentioned but not filled in. Use examples appropriate to your work. Keep any fixtures public only when they are synthetic or cleared for publication.</p>
  <h2>The release condition</h2>
  <p>Choose separate thresholds for usable completions, schema validity and task acceptance. If a wrong field is especially costly, make that failure a release blocker even when the average looks acceptable. Keep the reviewed examples and the exact schema version with the result.</p>
  <p>Your tracker can then say where an extractor is reliable enough for the job and where a person still needs to check it. A green parser alone cannot make that decision.</p>
  <PractitionerNote evidence={e} />
</InsightArticle>
